// Telegram Desktop palette template (§6b) -> os-rice.tdesktop-palette.
//
// Telegram paints its own widgets - it is not a GTK or Qt-themed app, so the
// GTK/Qt layers every other module installs do nothing to it. Its ONE theming
// mechanism is this file: a list of `constant: value;` lines, where a value is
// #rrggbb, #rrggbbaa, or the name of a constant defined earlier.
//
// Applying it is a manual click, once, and that is a Telegram limitation, not a
// gap here: the selected theme lives inside the encrypted tdata blob, so nothing
// outside the app can select it, and a file path on the command line is treated
// as a file to SEND, not a theme to load. Load it from inside the client instead
// (Settings -> Chat Settings -> "Choose from file", or send the file to Saved
// Messages and click it -> "Apply This Theme").
//
// One click total: a theme applied from a real path is watched
// (ChatBackground::refreshThemeWatcher) and re-applied on every change of that
// file, so a later `osr theme <name>` re-themes a running Telegram by itself.
//
// Constants left out fall back to Telegram's built-in DEFAULTS, which are the
// LIGHT scheme - hence the deliberately wide coverage below rather than a dozen
// base colors. The leading p* names are not Telegram keys: an unknown name is
// remembered and resolvable by later lines (that is how a palette declares its
// own variables), which is what keeps the theme roles in one block.
//
// Structure and key inventory follow the upstream Theme Reference
// (github.com/telegramdesktop/tdesktop/wiki/Theme-Reference).

// --- the theme's palette -----------------------------------------------------
pBg: {{background}};
pBgSunken: {{background_sunken}};
pPanel: {{panel_background}};
pMid: {{surface_mid}};
pSel: {{selection_background}};
pSelFg: {{selection_foreground}};
pFg: {{foreground}};
pDim: {{text_dim}};
pMuted: {{text_muted}};
pBorder: {{border}};
pAccent: {{accent}};
pRed: {{accent_red}};
pGreen: {{accent_green}};
pYellow: {{accent_yellow}};
pBlue: {{accent_blue}};
pMagenta: {{accent_magenta}};
pCyan: {{accent_cyan}};
pOrange: {{accent_orange}};
pError: {{error}};
pSuccess: {{success}};
pWarning: {{warning}};

// Alpha variants. A theme role is #rrggbb, and Telegram wants #rrggbbaa in the
// places that overlay something - so the alpha byte is appended to the rendered
// role right here rather than being a second palette entry per theme.
pShadow: {{background_sunken}}f0;
pLayerBg: {{background_sunken}}b0;
pRippleLight: {{foreground}}18;
pRippleHeavy: {{foreground}}30;
pSeparator: {{border}}60;
pServiceBg: {{surface_mid}}cc;
pServiceFg: {{foreground}}cc;
pDateImgBg: {{background_sunken}}99;
pMediaviewBg: {{background}}e6;
pAttentionOver: {{error}}30;

// Fully transparent. Written off a theme role rather than as #00000000 so this
// template holds no literal color at all - a hex here would be one theme's
// color leaking into every other theme's render (test/unit/theme_template.sh
// fails on exactly that).
cTransparent: {{background}}00;

